","ADHD\nAdjustment/Life Transitions\nAnxiety\nRelationship Issues\nStress Management\nYoung Adult Issues\nAnger Management\nAttachment\nCultural Concerns\nEmotional Regulation\nExpressive Arts Therapy\nFamily of Origin Issues\nGrief & Loss\nMindfulness\nSelf-Esteem/Self-Compassion\nSleep Issues/Insomnia\nSocial Skills Building\nTween/Teen Issues\nWomen's Issues","None","Career/Leadership\nCultural Concerns","Health Anxiety\nSocial Anxiety\nImmigration Hardship Evaluations\nCo-Parenting\nParent/Child Relationship\nParent Coaching\nNarcissistic Personality Disorder\nAdult Survivor of Narcissistic Parent\nPartner of Narcissist\nPerfectionism\nShame\nChristian-Based Counseling\nSpirituality\nReligious Trauma","","","","$135 . $160","","TRUE","\"a. I grew up in a Mexican American border community in South Texas\nb.White\nc. Spanish, german, irish and english\nd. Raised catholic; I am Christian and attend a non denominational church.\ne. heterosexual\nf. female\ng. married\nh. I have one 3-year-old child with my husband and co-parent my 9-year-old stepchild.\n\n\"","","",""],["Jessica W","Yes","Senior","Individuals","Adults (18 - 64)\nOlder Adults (65+)","North Austin/Spicewood Springs\nTelehealth","Virtual\nIn Person","","Aetna\nCurative\nNomi\nPrivate Pay","Adjustment/Life Transitions\nAnxiety\nAttachment\nChronic Illness/Chronic Pain\nDissociation/Dissociative Disorders\nEMDR\nEmotional Regulation\nExpressive Arts Therapy\nFamily of Origin Issues\nHighly Sensitive Persons (HSPs)\nMindfulness\nSelf-Esteem/Self-Compassion\nSomatic Experiencing (SE)\nStress Management\nTrauma\nBody Image/Eating & Food Concerns\nCaregiver Issues\nCultural Concerns\nDepression\nGender Identity Issues\nGrief & Loss\nLGBTQIA+\nWomen's Issues\nYoung Adult Issues","Health Anxiety\nSurvivor of DV/IPV\nSurvivor of Sexual Abuse\nSurvivor of Sexual Assault\nSandtray Therapy\nPerfectionism\nShame\nCertified SE Practitioner\nSomatic-Based Approaches\nSomatic Issues\nChildhood Abuse\nComplex-PTSD (C-PTSD)\nPost-Traumatic Stress Disorder (PTSD)\nSocial Anxiety\nHealth at Every Size (HAES)\nIntuitive Eating\nTransgender Issues\nNon-Monogamy (Consensual)\nSex Positive\nSpirituality\nFailure to Launch Concerns\nRacial Trauma\nPerimenopause\nPerinatal Mental Health\nPregnancy & Reproductive Issues\nPostpartum Issues","ADHD\nDeath & Dying\nMood Disorders\nSex & Sexuality\nSubstance Use/Addiction\nSuicidal Ideation","Parenting Child with ADHD\nPartner of Person with ADHD\nParenting Child with Autism\nParent/Child Relationship\nKink & Fetishes\nSelf-Injurious Behavior (w/o Suicidal Ideation)","","","","$165 . $190","","TRUE","I am a white, heterosexual, cis gendered lady who grew up in New England which folks definitely deserve to know as they are deciding who they feel comfortable working with. I am passionate about working with and supporting folks with differing intersections of identity than my own and am committed to doing my own ongoing work to unpack and challenge the harmful downloads that I have internalized due to my own social locations in the ongoing systems of oppression which I which I benefit from.\n\nI am also married and in a monogamous relationship and am a mama to a 21m old babe. ","I checked the somatic experiencing certified option for specialization because there was no option for Sensorimotor Psychotherapy which is a somatic modality for working with trauma and attachment issues that I have done extensive training in including the level 3 advanced integration training and becoming certified. There are not many people in Texas who are level 3 and certified. I would say the big difference between SE and SP (as I understand it since I'm not trained in somatic experiencing) is that SP honors the body/somatic experiences as an important source of information and place where we often hold trauma and deeper downloads about ourselves from attachment harms. AND SP is more holistic in that it works with emotions, thoughts/cognitions AND somatic/body based information to support healing and integration. Including a link to the SP website -if you scroll down to the \"What is Sensorimotor Psychotherapy\" they have a good description. https://sensorimotorpsychotherapy.org/about/. SP is my primary modality and orientation from which I work as it is extremely flexible and also very consent based. For many folks who have experienced/survived trauma and different harms in relationship the idea of working with the body can be quite scary (since the somatic/body level is often where we hold experiences/sensations that are too painful to be in connect with at all times). SO if you are talking to someone who is a little wary it could help to emphasize that my SP training (and my general orientation as a therapist and a human) really focuses on collaborating with folks around their working edge - so tracking and respecting what feels like too much - so honoring the person and their body/systems way of letting us know when they are out of their window of tolerance while also building the resources and regulatory capacities and consent needed to work with a sliver of what is activating so that change and ideally relief can occur. \n\nI am also trained in EMDR but I will say that depending on the persons experiences and symptoms I use this modality sparingly because I have found that it can sometimes lack the nuance a person needs around building safety and enough resources to work with challenging/traumatic content AND in being able to stay in their body/present moment experiences enough to integrate new information. Not that you need to get into all that with folks BUT it may be helpful to give a caveat for folks seeking EMDR specifically that my approach is to meet with them first to talk more about their current needs, difficulties, and goals/hopes and  so I can provide more info about my training and the ways in which I work and we can explore if EMDR or other trauma modalities make sense. \n\nThis is already quite a lot of words and I am not sure if I completely answered the question but I would say I specialize in working with somatic and other experiential modalities to offer a different experience (re hope/a pathway forward/a softer entry point) for folks who are either a bit dubious about therapy OR who have had past experiences in therapy and are wanting help shifting things on a deeper level than past talk therapy has allowed. \n\nSO if someone is only wanting verbal processing it may not be a good fit BUT if that is what they are most comfortable/familiar with ( verbal processing was my default for most of my life as well) but they are open to and maybe seeking something different that's GREAT.\n\nLastly, it's not uncommon for folks with more complex PTSD/trauma and difficulties with dissociation to also have some current or historical suicidal ideation and/or substance use. I have a lot of experience working with both and am open to working with someone who has a history of substance use and/or suicidal ideation and/or passive SI. Anyone with recent attempts or current severe substance use would require a different level of care for stabilization and safety support and would not be a good fit esp as I have a young babe at home and am trying to achieve some work life balance:) Please let me know if that all makes sense and also please feel free to reach out to me if any of this feels unhelpful or unclear or as referrals arise. \n","","Alyssa Test copy"],["Caitlin","Yes","Mid.level","Individuals","Teens (13-18)\nAdults (18 - 64)\nOlder Adults (65+)","North Austin/Spicewood Springs\nTelehealth","Virtual\nIn Person","","Aetna\nCurative\nEHN/WebTPA\nHarbor Health\nNomi\nSana\nPrivate Pay","Adjustment/Life Transitions\nAnxiety\nDepression\nEMDR\nGender Identity Issues\nGrief & Loss\nLGBTQIA+\nSex & Sexuality\nTrauma\nWomen's Issues\nYoung Adult Issues\nAcceptance & Commitment Therapy (ACT)\nADHD\nAnger Management\nAttachment\nAutism\nCareer/Leadership\nCaregiver Issues\nCognitive Behav